WEBVTT

00:03.760 --> 00:07.160
Okay guys, let's say that in our data we have the following informations.

00:07.160 --> 00:09.280
We have the months and the sales.

00:09.520 --> 00:13.320
Now if you apply any aggregate functions in SQL what can happen.

00:13.360 --> 00:18.720
SQL can go through all rows of the window or the entire data and start aggregating the data.

00:18.840 --> 00:24.920
So that means in the result in the output, SQL is going to give you one single aggregated value.

00:25.000 --> 00:27.600
SQL is going to go and summarize all those values.

00:27.600 --> 00:32.960
And in the output you're going to find for example here the total sales is going to be 175.

00:33.000 --> 00:35.720
Or you can use the average or count the data and so on.

00:35.840 --> 00:41.840
So the aggregate functions are going to deliver at the end one aggregated value for a window or for

00:41.840 --> 00:42.960
the entire data.

00:43.000 --> 00:43.200
Okay.

00:43.240 --> 00:46.920
So now let's have a quick overview of the syntax of all aggregate functions.

00:46.920 --> 00:48.920
Most of them follow the same rule.

00:48.920 --> 00:51.560
So first as usual we have to define the function name.

00:51.560 --> 00:53.240
And in this example we have the average.

00:53.240 --> 00:59.200
Then to the next part we have to define inside it as well the expression we cannot leave it empty.

00:59.200 --> 01:05.070
So here we are using the sales and the second rule for all functions beside the count, the data type

01:05.070 --> 01:07.030
of this field should be a number.

01:07.030 --> 01:08.350
And this of course makes sense, right?

01:08.390 --> 01:12.670
So we cannot find the average of the first name of customers or something like that.

01:12.670 --> 01:14.150
So we have to define a number.

01:14.150 --> 01:16.270
Then next we have to define the frame.

01:16.270 --> 01:18.990
So we have the partition pi and it is optional.

01:18.990 --> 01:20.310
So you could use it or leave.

01:20.310 --> 01:21.070
It depends.

01:21.070 --> 01:24.030
And then the next one we have the order by it is as well optional.

01:24.030 --> 01:25.870
It is not a must or required.

01:25.870 --> 01:27.510
So you could use it or leave it.

01:27.510 --> 01:32.230
That's been the whole definition of the window could be empty for the aggregate functions.

01:32.230 --> 01:33.710
Let's have a look to all functions.

01:33.710 --> 01:36.670
So we have the count sum average min max.

01:36.830 --> 01:41.870
And as you can see, only the count accepts all data types as an expression or arguments.

01:41.910 --> 01:45.270
All others require you to have a number as a data type.

01:45.270 --> 01:47.990
And for all functions the partition by is optional.

01:48.030 --> 01:49.670
The same for order by and frame.

01:49.670 --> 01:51.910
So everything is optional over here.

01:51.910 --> 01:55.630
So now what we're going to do is that we're going to go and deep dive into each of those functions in

01:55.630 --> 01:59.110
order to understand how they work, what are the use cases.

01:59.150 --> 02:00.950
And of course we're going to practice in SQL.

02:00.950 --> 02:03.710
So we're going to start with the first one with the function count.
